#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97022149-aafb-5442-ac02-22e0b9bb0631" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4g^{-1}(x) + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117295} {:answer/id #uuid "df7641dd-fe68-5b9e-8570-c7f585537212" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2g^{-1}(x) + 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117296} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0d8fbd-9319-5cd2-808d-f79220c295ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (1)/(4g^{-1}(x) + 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117297} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd940ad-4f3a-5178-a721-73177b027dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2(g^{-1}(x)) + 6g^{-1}(x) - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117298} {:answer/id #uuid "4a77d884-01d5-50a0-a895-4773e3a434a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4g^{-1}(x) + 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a77d884-01d5-50a0-a895-4773e3a434a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(4g^{-1}(x) + 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117299} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5f56a4-0cea-562e-ad02-bfdf87c44b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e6ddbb-e71d-53d4-9a83-95cdcae2804b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32629" :question/problem "If $g(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+6x-2,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+6y-2\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+6y-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y+6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:**\n\nLastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{g}^{-1}(x)+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad925306-6ea8-5e2c-9e3b-32f9e2df144d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283755} {:answer/id #uuid "89a7fa6b-b50e-5746-969c-6acd383bb954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283756} {:answer/id #uuid "a837a5c4-7c35-5567-996e-102de7481178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb0f1bf-4173-5ab0-84e3-559c9e506b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283758} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f4e640-afad-56d1-8164-1b599e04f178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f4e640-afad-56d1-8164-1b599e04f178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283759} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce74b2bf-612a-5baa-96d1-bf8cd8afd5c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4703ff8b-5a2b-5e85-8bf3-fcdffdf5accb" :question/math-academy-id "q-3300" :question/problem "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c0b7d95-c699-5762-aa44-449712f035c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Polynomial Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aea41ae-407f-5ea2-a1d0-7fd2faa360fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{f}^{-1}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283762} {:answer/id #uuid "2823ab8a-a6b5-5f48-9d2a-109a82e800a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{f}^{-1}(x)}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283763} {:answer/id #uuid "42115277-45df-5ed3-a939-9e1c8adda727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283764} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0c894-812c-56ab-8eb1-2ee377463d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283765} {:answer/id #uuid "36f1505d-dbf1-5581-8b11-1c7127e55f04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae0c894-812c-56ab-8eb1-2ee377463d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283765} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4aceb8e-29ac-5dac-88ed-3048ee820a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f49ac9a8-95f1-5717-9214-352b4ebc352c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3302" :question/problem "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+9,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+9,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe7b751-b900-5083-8d6e-8a7f5912f219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283769} {:answer/id #uuid "4780b023-f771-5749-bc7f-80a0bb9ab5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283770} {:answer/id #uuid "48d64e71-651e-5a81-a0ee-9a97026a0dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283771} {:answer/id #uuid "3e6b1d43-1654-5781-89ab-f9d2b6071942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283772} {:answer/id #uuid "e4eeda2c-2ca5-5450-a593-be06143a83cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4780b023-f771-5749-bc7f-80a0bb9ab5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283770} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b1126e6-9b33-54d9-9939-f85fd79daba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec72463d-d5ee-5538-ac45-df3dfce269a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-51349" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}),$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}),$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{y})\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{y})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y})\\cdot {e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{y})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & ={e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}) \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{-{f}^{-1}(x)}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({e}^{{f}^{-1}(x)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ddc6a5d-078d-5f74-913a-1b783cf9c176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283776} {:answer/id #uuid "2b44b690-a257-53dd-b0e9-08d6e65ee33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡({g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({g}^{-1}(x))}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283777} {:answer/id #uuid "cc27fa82-f77e-5bb0-9944-88b097bb375f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283778} {:answer/id #uuid "d415f90d-931a-5364-ac4b-a60169672a20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({g}^{-1}(x))}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283779} {:answer/id #uuid "1ededc11-a2c6-5946-be23-ecd02932ca72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({g}^{-1}(x))}{{g}^{-1}(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ddc6a5d-078d-5f74-913a-1b783cf9c176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283776} :answer-field/id #uuid "737544f4-ede3-5246-8d01-81e278ac43a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21913596-666f-5e3b-b3cf-ebdaeea1672d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73083" :question/problem "If $g(x)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\operatorname{sec}⁡2y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2{g}^{-1}(x))}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
